// Formative practice quiz for what-makes-a-component-re-render. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/effects-routing-and-rendering/what-makes-a-component-re-render

::what-makes-a-component-re-render o1 - two causes::Which pair lists two causes of a component re-rendering, as taught so far?{
~A change to a local variable, and a CSS change#Not quite. Neither of these asks React to render.
~A scroll, and a mouse move#Not quite. These only cause a render if a handler sets state.
~Every fetch request#Not quite. A fetch does not render anything until a response sets state.
=A state update in it, and its parent re-rendering#Correct. These are the two causes this topic teaches.
}

::what-makes-a-component-re-render o2 - render is a call::What does rendering a component actually mean in React?{
~The browser repaints the whole page#Not quite. Painting comes later, and only for what changed.
~All DOM nodes are destroyed and rebuilt#Not quite. React changes only what differs.
=React calls your component function to get its output#Correct. Rendering is the calculation step.
~The component is removed from the page#Not quite. That is unmounting, not rendering.
}

::what-makes-a-component-re-render o2 - commit differences::A list of 100 rows re-renders, and one row has a new value. What does React change in the DOM?{
=Only what differs in that row#Correct. React compares output and commits the differences.
~All 100 rows#Not quite. All the functions may run, but unchanged DOM is left alone.
~Nothing, because re-renders do not touch the DOM#Not quite. The changed value does need a DOM update.
~The whole page#Not quite. The page is not rebuilt.
}

::what-makes-a-component-re-render o3 - predict tree::App contains Sidebar and Main, and Main contains Chart. Main updates its state. Which components render?{
~Only Main#Not quite. Its children render too, by default.
=Main and Chart#Correct. A render flows down from the component that updated.
~All of them#Not quite. Parents and siblings are not affected.
~App and Main#Not quite. A render does not flow upward.
}

::what-makes-a-component-re-render o3 - memo role::What is memo for?{
=A performance optimisation that can skip a render when props are unchanged#Correct. Your code must still work correctly without it.
~A guarantee that a component never renders twice#Not quite. React may still render it, and state or context changes do.
~Fixing bugs caused by extra renders#Not quite. Those bugs should be fixed in the code itself.
~Saving state between page reloads#Not quite. memo has nothing to do with persistence.
}
